\chapter{Introduction}

The \texttt{cl-coingecko} library is a Common Lisp client for the CoinGecko
\textsc{Api}~\cite{coingecko-docs}.  It is \emph{not} an official CoinGecko
product; it is an independent wrapper around their public \textsc{Rest}
surface.

The thin layer lives in the \texttt{coingecko/rest-api} package: one function
per documented \texttt{GET} path, plus shared authentication, query encoding,
and a sixty-second \texttt{function-cache}.  The \texttt{coingecko} package
re-exports that layer and adds the helpers I actually call from
\url{https://limbic.fi} --- current quotes and price histories for Bitcoin
and friends, including gold (\texttt{xau}) and silver (\texttt{xag}).

The library covers the v3 \textsc{Rest} endpoints from CoinGecko's Open\textsc{Api}
specification~\cite{coingecko-openapi}: simple prices, coin metadata,
historical charts and \textsc{Ohlc}, exchanges, derivatives, treasuries,
\textsc{Nft}s, search, global stats, and the onchain / GeckoTerminal paths.
WebSockets and webhooks are not wrapped.

You can download the library from GitHub at:\\
\url{https://github.com/cgore/cl-coingecko}

\section*{Getting Lisp}

Before using this library you need a working Lisp.  I use and recommend
\textsc{Sbcl}, Steel Bank Common Lisp, which is available at:\\
\url{http://www.sbcl.org}

\textsc{SBCL} has information on getting started at:\\
\url{http://www.sbcl.org/getting.html}\\
If you are using Debian or a similar Linux distribution (including Ubuntu), you
can just run as root:\\
\texttt{apt-get install sbcl sbcl-doc sbcl-source}

\section*{Getting \textsc{Emacs} and \textsc{Slime}}

After installing, the best way to interact with any Common Lisp is via
\textsc{Slime}, the Superior Lisp Interaction Mode for \textsc{Emacs}, which is
available at:\\
\url{http://common-lisp.net/project/slime/}\\
This can be installed on Debian by:\\
\texttt{apt-get install slime emacs emacs-goodies-el}

\section*{Using the Library}

Clone the repository next to your other local systems, or symlink the
\textsc{Asdf} file into a directory on \texttt{asdf:*central-registry*}
(I use \texttt{\textasciitilde/programming/lisp/systems/}).

\begin{lstlisting}
mkdir -p ~/programming/lisp
cd ~/programming/lisp
git clone git@github.com:cgore/cl-coingecko.git
ln -s ~/programming/lisp/cl-coingecko/coingecko.asd \
      ~/programming/lisp/systems/
\end{lstlisting}

The system depends on \texttt{alexandria}, \texttt{dexador},
\texttt{function-cache}, \texttt{quri}, \texttt{sigma}, and \texttt{yason}.
Those come from Quicklisp.

\begin{lstlisting}
(ql:quickload :coingecko)
(in-package :coingecko)
(price-history :bitcoin :vs-currency :usd :days 365)
(btc/usd)
\end{lstlisting}

\section*{Authentication}

CoinGecko currently has three \textsc{Rest} plans.  The library picks one at
load time from the process environment, or you can switch in the image.

\begin{tabular}{llp{0.38\textwidth}}
\toprule
Plan & Host & Header \\
\midrule
Keyless & public v3 & none \\
Demo & public v3 & \texttt{x-cg-demo-api-key} \\
Pro & Pro v3 & \texttt{x-cg-pro-api-key} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

The public host is \texttt{https://api.coingecko.com/api/v3}.
The Pro host is \texttt{https://pro-api.coingecko.com/api/v3}.

On load it reads, in order, \texttt{COINGECKO\_PRO\_API\_KEY},
\texttt{COINGECKO\_DEMO\_API\_KEY}, then \texttt{COINGECKO\_API\_KEY}.  You
can also call \texttt{use-demo}, \texttt{use-pro}, or \texttt{use-keyless}.

Keyless is fine for trying things.  It is rate-limited (roughly 10--30
calls per minute, shared by \textsc{Ip}) and is not suitable for production
polling.  Get a Demo or Pro key from the CoinGecko developer dashboard.

Some endpoints --- \textsc{Ohlc} range, new listings, news, most onchain
filters, enterprise supply charts --- require a paid plan.  The wrappers
are still there; CoinGecko will reject them if your key cannot call them.

\section*{Testing}

Specs are \texttt{sigma/behave} \texttt{behavior} / \texttt{should} forms,
the same style as $\Sigma$.  Pure helpers run at load time.
\texttt{asdf:test-system} reloads the sources so those assertions run
again.

\begin{lstlisting}
(asdf:test-system :coingecko)
\end{lstlisting}

To also hit the live \textsc{Api} (ping, a Bitcoin price, and a one-day
history), set \texttt{COINGECKO\_LIVE\_TESTS=1} in the environment first.
A Demo key is enough; keyless works if you have not already exhausted the
public rate limit.

Have fun!
